  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nExpansive high pressure centered over the mid-Appalachians \nwhile a prolonged NE-SW oriented cold front from south-central \nCanada into the desert Southwest CONUS is keeping pronounced \nwarm/moist flow into the Upper Midwest. This is partially \nevidenced by the extensive low stratus shield over much of MN \ninto the Northern-Central Plains this afternoon while \ntemperatures are still expected to rise into the lower 50s for \nmuch of the WFO MPX coverage area for highs today.\n\nThe aforementioned front will slowly slide east tonight through \ntomorrow, crossing the Dakotas/NE/KS, helping keep clouds in place \nbut also continue the warm air advection into the region. In \naddition, breezy/gusty winds will persist as the approaching front \nwill tighten the pressure gradient between the eastern seaboard high \npressure and the incoming front. The front will push through the \narea on Thursday, with no precipitation for our area (but with some \nrain showers for far northern MN) and also very little change in \ntemperatures behind the frontal passage. Highs on Wednesday ahead of \nthe front will nudge into the mid 50s to lower 60s followed by \ndropping back into 50s area-wide on Thursday. This cooldown will be \nbrief as another surge of warm air advection is expected for Friday \nas the passing cold front retreats back north as a warm front \nattendant from a potent low pressure system developing over WY. This \nlow will quickly shift into northern MN by Saturday morning with its \ntrailing cold front extending SW over MN/IA/NE/KS. A sharp increase \nin rain chances is expected with this Saturday frontal passage, \nbringing widespread rain showers to the area. Even though periods of \nrain can be expected from late Friday through late Saturday, \nrainfall amounts will overall not be much. Only around a quarter \ninch of rain is expected.\n\nHowever, the bigger impact will be a sharp drop in temperatures on \nthe backside of this cold front as north-central Canadian-origin \nhigh pressure will drop into the region starting late Saturday and \nexpand over the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes through the first half \nof next week. Highs will plunge into the 30s starting Sunday while \nlows go from the 40s-50s early Saturday morning to the 20s-30s early \nSunday morning, and remain in the 20s through at least the first \nhalf of next week. As such, as the precipitation wraps up Saturday \nnight into early Sunday morning, some snowflakes may mix in with the \nrain showers. So, while this dramatic drop in temperatures may seem \nabnormal, the only abnormal part is the warmth we are currently \ndealing with, and the nearly 20-degree drop will bring temperatures \nback to near normal for later November. There are even some \nindications that this cold advection surge may not last. Climate \nPrediction Center outlooks for the end of November into early \nDecember still show the Upper Midwest as solidly into Above Normal \nTemperatures. This does not necessarily mean \"warm\" since our \nnormals are continuing to drop going through fall, but does mean \nthat temperatures will still run relatively mild for the time of \nyear. In addition, these same outlooks point to Below Normal \nPrecipitation, and add credence to the forecast that we do not have \nany significant snow systems for our area anytime soon.",
